; ============================================================
; ELIZA for the 1802 — STELLAR   *** v2: EXPANDED SCRIPT ***
; ============================================================
; 35 keyword rules (was 10) and 13 pronoun-swap pairs (was 3).
; Uses PRINTSTR ($C1 0X) for the goodbye and the fallback line — one opcode
; prints a whole null-terminated string, replacing a 17-byte char loop each.
;
; *** TYPE IN CAPITALS *** (auto-upper is still to do.)
;
; A $01 inside a reply splices in the fragment that followed the keyword, with
; pronoun swapping applied at word boundaries (YOU<->I, MY<->YOUR, ARE<->AM,
; ME->YOU, WAS<->WERE, MYSELF<->YOURSELF, I'M->YOU ARE, YOU'RE->I AM).
;
; Memory:
;   $4000..        code (ends F0 00 00)
;   $4700 greeting   $4770 fallback   $4790 goodbye
;   $4800..$4C91  keyword table  [KEYWORD\0][REPLY\0]... , extra \0 ends it
;   $4D00..$4D95  swap table     [FROM\0][TO\0]... , FROM has a leading space
;   $5000..       INPUT buffer
;
; Rebuild with Firmware-Math/build_eliza.py (work repo) — it regenerates the
; tables from a Python list, so adding rules does not touch the code.
;
; KNOWN, PRE-EXISTING: the first character prints twice ("EELIZA"). The program
; is entered a second time from $4000 shortly after start, so whatever has been
; emitted by then repeats. It is NOT caused by PRINTSTR — but PRINTSTR makes it
; far more visible, because collapsing the greeting from a many-iteration loop
; into ONE opcode lets execution reach the prompt and the first SERIN before the
; re-entry. That is why the greeting deliberately still uses the char loop.
; ============================================================
